{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed262781723/16/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d6542281\" lines=\"72-91\">\n<description>\nComplete each partial marker mask by stamping the matching multicolor sprite over it.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background contains one or more 8-connected multicolor sprites.\n- Separate target masks use colors that already occur inside one of those sprites.\n- A target mask may contain only one marker pixel or several same-alignment marker pixels.\n\nRule\n- For each multicolor sprite, test every translation that aligns a target pixel with a\n  same-color sprite pixel.\n- Keep the translations with maximal exact overlap against the existing target pixels,\n  rejecting translations that conflict with other foreground or overlap a source sprite.\n- Stamp the whole sprite at those translations; if a non-marker sprite color occurs as\n  a single source pixel, consume that source-only marker back to background.\n\nKey insight\nThe scattered target pixels are partial copies: their same-color overlap with a sprite\ndetermines where the full sprite must be completed.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3a25b0d8\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes in a target object with colors from a source object's subsprites, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Two similar 8-connected objects share an outline color.\n- Source has colored subsprites in cavities; target has empty cavities.\n\nRule:\n- Find the two 8-connected objects.\n- Target = object with more hole area. Align subsprites to holes (rotation/flip if needed).\n- Fill each hole with its matched subsprite color; tight-crop.\n\nKey insight: Hole centers in the target are matched to source subsprite centers under the best rotation/flip transform.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ad3b40cf\" lines=\"72-87\">\n<description>\nMirror the smallest non-axis foreground color across the full straight axis.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One foreground color forms exactly one full row, column, main diagonal, or anti-diagonal reflection axis.\n- The remaining foreground colors include a unique least-populated color to copy.\n\nRule\n- Preserve the input grid.\n- Select every pixel of the unique smallest non-axis foreground color.\n- Reflect those pixels across the full straight axis and paint the reflected positions with the same color.\n\nKey insight\n- The axis color marks the mirror line, while the color population chooses which set of objects is duplicated.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a09f6c25\" lines=\"33-50\">\n<description>\nRecolor each non-singleton red object by its symmetry axis and erase isolated red pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color and red 4-connected foreground objects.\n- Every non-singleton red object has exactly one reflection symmetry category:\n  horizontal, vertical, or diagonal.\n- Single red pixels are noise.\n\nRule:\n- Start from an all-background output of the same shape.\n- Recolor horizontally symmetric red objects blue, vertically symmetric objects\n  green, and diagonally symmetric objects pink.\n- Leave isolated red pixels as background.\n\nKey insight: The target color is not tied to object position or size; it is a\nlabel for the object's reflection symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5792cb4d\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nReverse the sequence of colors along the single 4-connected foreground path while keeping the path shape fixed.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- All non-background pixels form one multicolor 4-connected one-pixel-wide path.\n- The path has exactly two endpoints and no branches.\n\nRule:\n- Walk the path from one endpoint to the other.\n- Replace the colors on those same path cells by the original path colors in reverse order.\n- Leave every background pixel unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- Nothing moves geometrically; only the color string written on the path is reversed between the endpoints.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8dab14c2\" lines=\"120-133\">\n<description>\nComplete each boundary tab or notch by adding the complementary feature on the opposite side of the foreground object.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has exactly two colors: a dominant background and one foreground object.\n- The foreground object is a thick rectilinear shape with one-pixel tabs and notches on its boundary.\n\nRule\n- A foreground tab is a one-neighbor boundary pixel; project through its full row or column span and remove the opposite edge pixel to make a matching notch.\n- A background notch is a one-pixel cut with three foreground 4-neighbors; project across the full row or column span and add a tab just outside the opposite edge.\n- Notches immediately beside a tab source are not independent sources, because that gap belongs to the tab's local boundary shape.\n\nKey insight\n- The changed pixels are not color-specific noise; they are paired jigsaw-like boundary features on opposite sides of the same thick row or column cross-section.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a416fc5b\" lines=\"95-106\">\n<description>\nAdd the two missing colored arms of a centered diamond-sprite motif, or clear a completed motif to a blank square.\n\nInput structure\nAn 11x11 board has orange background and pink separator bars forming nine 3x3 cells. Occupied cells contain a four-pixel diamond sprite; the center cell and one neighboring cell are red, and a completed board may also contain one grey and one teal sprite.\n\nRule\nWith only the two red sprites present, preserve the board and add grey and teal diamond sprites 135 degrees clockwise and counterclockwise from the non-center red arm. If those expected grey and teal sprites are already present, output an all-background square whose side length is the total number of sprite pixels.\n\nKey insight\nThe non-center red sprite is an eight-neighbor direction from the center; the other two colored arms are fixed rotations of that direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f8f52ecc\" lines=\"107-122\">\n<description>\nConnect same-colored foreground components with one-cell-wide orthogonal paths around teal wall segments.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Teal cells are fixed wall segments and are never source connectors.\n- Other foreground colors appear as one or more 4-connected components; colors with a single component stay unchanged.\n\nRule\n- For each non-background, non-teal color, read its 4-connected components in row-major order.\n- Connect each consecutive pair by the shortest clear L-shaped path between the two components.\n- Clear paths may pass only through background or the same color, and they preserve the previous travel axis when both bends are possible.\n\nKey insight\n- The output is not a global recolor: each color traces a routed chain through its own components while teal cells and other colors act as blockers.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"337b420f\" lines=\"41-55\">\n<description>\nKeep the larger sprite from each separated panel and overlay those sprites into one 5x5 canvas.\n\nInput structure:\n- The input is a 5x17 grid: three 5x5 panels separated by one-cell black columns.\n- Each panel has the same background color and exactly one foreground color split into two 4-connected components.\n\nRule:\n- In each panel, discard the smaller foreground component and keep the unique largest component as the sprite.\n- Process panels left to right, placing each sprite at its panel coordinates on a 5x5 background canvas.\n- If a sprite would cover an already placed sprite, slide it by the unique nearest in-bounds offset that avoids overlap.\n\nKey insight:\n- The extra same-colored component in each panel is a distractor; the final answer is the non-overlapping overlay of the three largest components.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fc10701f\" lines=\"20-35\">\n<description>\nMove the orange rectangle onto the maroon rectangle and mark gated corridor crossings in red.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant background contains black obstacle segments.\n- There is one solid orange rectangle and one solid maroon rectangle of the same size.\n- The two rectangles share either their row span or their column span, so the move is straight and axis-aligned.\n\nRule\n- Erase the original orange rectangle to the background.\n- Replace the maroon rectangle with the orange rectangle.\n- Between the two rectangles, paint red across the moving rectangle's width or height only at corridor cells flanked by black obstacle segments perpendicular to the movement.\n\nKey insight\n- The black segments define gates in the straight corridor; only those gated openings, not every swept background cell, become red.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"45a5af55\" lines=\"5-18\">\n<description>\nBuild nested square frames from a stack of horizontal color stripes.\n\nInput structure:\n- Full-width horizontal stripes of distinct non-bg colors, stacked vertically.\n- Each stripe has uniform color and variable height (1-4 rows).\n\nRule:\n- Read stripes bottom to top.\n- Bottom stripe becomes the center solid square with side length = stripe height.\n- Each subsequent stripe wraps the current shape with a square frame of thickness = stripe height, in that stripe's color.\n\nKey insight: Stripe height doubles as both frame thickness and (for the innermost) center-square side length; output dimensions are fully determined by the stripe heights.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"689c358e\" lines=\"72-88\">\n<description>\nMark each arrow-like sprite's pointed frame endpoint with its color and the opposite endpoint black.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a one-pixel solid rectangular frame around a dominant interior background.\n- Inside the frame are monochrome six-pixel sprites shaped as asymmetric T arrows.\n- Each sprite has a length-4 bar and a length-3 crossbar one cell closer to the pointed end.\n\nRule:\n- For each sprite, follow its bar axis from the shorter end to the frame.\n- Paint that frame endpoint with the sprite color.\n- Paint the opposite endpoint on the same row or column black.\n- Leave the interior background, sprites, and all other frame pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The direction is encoded by the crossbar's off-center position: the nearer end is the pointing end.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a644e277\" lines=\"31-50\">\n<description>\nExtract the lattice subregion bounded by the four background-colored corner holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- A regular one-cell-thick lattice has one line color, one background color,\n  and sparse marker pixels.\n- Exactly four lattice-line intersections are background-colored holes instead\n  of line-color pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Find the unique non-background color that forms regular horizontal and\n  vertical lattice lines.\n- Use the four background-colored lattice intersections as the corners of one\n  rectangle.\n- Return the inclusive crop between those corner rows and columns.\n\nKey insight:\n- The crop is marked by the missing lattice intersections, not by the marker\n  pixels inside the cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"5545f144\" lines=\"191-207\">\n<description>\nMove a triangular sprite across the grid, consuming aligned marker pixels.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
